{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2512.11931, what are two limitations of the evidence scan and how could they be addressed?", "answer": "The scan excluded non-English documents and sector-specific mitigations; a systematic review with a wider expert panel could address these.", "evidence": [{"page": 13, "quote": "It also excluded documents not in English and did not consider mitigations for AI risks in specific sectors, or for specific use cases."}, {"page": 13, "quote": "These limitations could be meaningfully addressed by a systematic review of AI risk mitigations. In such a review, a wider panel of experts could help identify and classify a comprehensive set of documents to increase rigor while maintaining practicality for research end-users."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2512.11931, what are the four main categories of the preliminary AI Risk Mitigation Taxonomy and what is one example of a subcategory from each?", "answer": "The four categories are Governance & Oversight, Technical & Security, Operational Process, and Transparency & Accountability. Examples: Board Structure & Oversight, Model Alignment, Testing & Auditing, System Documentation.", "evidence": [{"page": 17, "quote": "| 1. Governance & Oversight Mitigations Formal organizational structures and policy frameworks that establish human oversight mechanisms and decision protocols to ensure human accountability, ethical conduct, and risk management throughout AI development and deployment. | 1.1 Board Structure & Oversight 1.2 Risk Management 1.3 Conflict of Interest Protections 1.4 Whistleblower Reporting & Protection 1.5 Safety Decision Frameworks 1.6 Environmental Impact Management 1.7 Societal Impact Assessment |"}, {"page": 18, "quote": "| 2. Technical & Security Mitigations Technical, physical, and engineering safeguards that secure AI systems and constrain model behaviors to ensure security, safety, alignment with human values, and content integrity. | 2.1 Model & Infrastructure Security 2.2 Model Alignment 2.3 Model Safety Engineering 2.4 Content Safety Controls |"}, {"page": 19, "quote": "| 3. Operational Process Mitigations Processes and management frameworks governing AI system deployment, usage, monitoring, incident handling, and validation, which promote safety, security, and accountability throughout the system lifecycle. | 3.1 Testing & Auditing 3.2 Data Governance 3.3 Access Management 3.4 Staged Deployment 3.5 Post-Deployment Monitoring 3.6 Incident Response & Recovery |"}, {"page": 20, "quote": "| 4. Transparency & Accountability Mitigations Formal disclosure practices and verification mechanisms that communicate AI system information and enable external scrutiny to build trust, facilitate oversight, and ensure accountability to users, regulators, and the public. | 4.1 System Documentation 4.2 Risk Disclosure 4.3 Incident Reporting 4.4 Governance Disclosure 4.5 Third-Party System Access 4.6 User Rights & Recourse |"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2512.11931, what does the abbreviation 'RLHF' stand for in the context of Technical & Security Mitigations?", "answer": "'RLHF' stands for Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "Reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), direct preference optimization (DPO), constitutional AI training, value alignment verification systems"}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2512.11931, what is the definition of 'Operational Process Mitigations'?", "answer": "Operational Process Mitigations are processes and management frameworks governing AI system deployment, usage, monitoring, incident handling, and validation, which promote safety, security, and accountability throughout the system lifecycle.", "evidence": [{"page": 19, "quote": "| 3. Operational Process Mitigations Processes and management frameworks governing AI system deployment, usage, monitoring, incident handling, and validation, which promote safety, security, and accountability throughout the system lifecycle. |"}]},
  {"question": "Which mitigation in arXiv 2512.11931 involves restricting model release practices to avoid exaggerated claims?", "answer": "Avoiding hype involves AGI labs not releasing models in ways likely to create hype, such as overstating results.", "evidence": [{"page": 21, "quote": "| Avoiding hype                            | AGI labs should avoid releasing models in ways likely to create hype (e.g., by overstating results)                     | Schuett et al (2023)            |"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2512.11931, which mitigation subcategory includes anonymous reporting channels and non-retaliation guarantees?", "answer": "Whistleblower Reporting & Protection includes anonymous reporting channels and non-retaliation guarantees.", "evidence": [{"page": 17, "quote": "Policies and systems that enable confidential reporting of safety concerns or ethical violations to prevent retaliation and encourage disclosure of risks. Protocols and commitments that constrain decision- making about model development, deployment, and capability scaling, and govern safety-capability resource allocation to prevent unsafe AI advancement. ... Anonymous reporting channels, non-retaliation guarantees, limitations on non-disparagement agreements, external whistleblower handling services"}]}
]}
